  "research_id": "banking-money-pipes-refresh-2026-10-12",
  "title": "What changed in banking and the money pipes after the Site build",
  "question": "What materially changed in banks, payment rails, clearing and tokenized settlement, and how do ACH, Securitize Stocks/USDC and DTC tokenized entitlements differ across capture, clearing, settlement, finality, availability, redemption, correction and loss?",
  "bluf": "ACH settles bank obligations at the Fed; Securitize trades a broker entitlement against USDC; DTC moves an incumbent participant entitlement free of value. Securitize has the stronger public launch receipt, DTC the stronger public correction constitution, and neither token movement merges title, cash, finality, correction and loss into one event.",
  "evidence_cutoff": "2026-10-18T23:59:59-04:00",
  "evidence_cutoff_note": "Direct-site banking and infrastructure restart from the July bank portraits and the October 2 federal monetary cutoff, extended on October 18 only for the OCC nine-bank fair-access review and the separate American Express and Bank of America enforcement/control collision. The ACH, Securitize and DTC findings retain their October 12 clocks. The package records source-specific baselines and does not claim an exhaustive bank-site census. Federal Register publication does not substitute for operating terms, directories, notices, transaction records or reconciliation evidence.",
  "use_note": "Use for the bounded October banking restart, ACH and Securitize traces, Securitize-versus-DTC comparison, four money-language maps and governed Settlement Language Lab. Follow stable architecture to the bank, Fed/Treasury/clearing and Crypto Hub owners. Keep customer claim, bank book, operator file, settlement account, broker/depository entitlement, chain state, issuer register, stablecoin redemption, correction and loss separate.",
